# Introduction to Trees {#sec-tr}

::: {.callout-note title="Sources"}
**`tree_book` ch. 1** (five lessons, six patterns) · **CLRS** ch. 10 (trees), ch. B.5 (graphs) · **CPH** ch. 14 (tree algorithms, the rooted view) · **CP-Algorithms** *Tree basics*, *Depth and height* · LeetCode binary-tree explore cards.

**Coverage:** the six equivalent definitions of a tree · rooted vocabulary (parent, ancestor, depth, height, subtree, leaf) and the edge-count versus node-count trap · full, complete, perfect, balanced · storing a tree (pointers, edge list, parent array, children lists, heap indexes, LeetCode level order) · the base / trust / combine recursion, top-down and bottom-up · reading contest input · six patterns: measure a subtree, compare or mirror two trees, carry state down a root-to-leaf path, parent arrays, degrees and leaves on an unrooted tree, root an edge list at the vertex the question is about.
:::

::: {.callout-tip title="What you will be able to do"}
- Decide whether an edge list is a tree, and say which of the six definitions you used.
- Compute depth, height, size and distance, and switch between the edge convention and LeetCode's node convention without an off-by-one.
- Store the same tree as pointers, an edge list, a parent array or children lists, and convert in $O(n)$.
- Write a tree recursion whose base case, recursive call and combination you can point at, and replace it with a loop when the height can be $n$.
- Recognise which of the six patterns a statement is asking for before writing code.
:::

This chapter opens the Trees volume. It builds on two things you already have: recursion (a function that solves a smaller copy of its own problem) and the cost analysis of @sec-complexity, where the maximum depth of a binary tree first appeared. Everything here — the words, the storage formats, the three-line recursion — is what @sec-tv and every later tree chapter assume.


## Why this matters

A tree is the one shape in which every question about the whole splits into the same question about the parts. There is exactly one path between two vertices, so a subtree is a tree, a root-to-leaf walk never branches back, and deleting one edge cuts the graph in two. Interviews hand you that shape as a `TreeNode`. Contests hand you $n - 1$ edges, or a parent $p_i$, and expect you to notice that it is the same shape.

This chapter is the vocabulary and the six small ideas that already solve a large fraction of tree problems: measure the subtrees, compare two of them, walk down from the root carrying state, follow parent pointers, read degrees and leaves with no root at all, or choose the root yourself. Later chapters add traversals, binary search trees, lowest common ancestors and heavy-light decomposition. They all assume the words and the recursion from here.

Two counting conventions live side by side, and mixing them is the most common off-by-one in this chapter. In this book, **depth and height count edges**: the root has depth $0$, a leaf has height $0$, and an empty tree has height $-1$. LeetCode's "maximum depth" counts **nodes**: an empty tree is $0$ and a single node is $1$. The same tree is one larger in the LeetCode number. The sample answer tells you which convention the problem uses.

## Lesson 1: what a tree is {#sec-tr-what}

A **tree** is an undirected graph that is connected and acyclic. On $n \ge 1$ vertices it has exactly $n - 1$ edges. A graph that is acyclic but possibly disconnected is a **forest**; a forest of $c$ trees on $n$ vertices has $n - c$ edges.

For $n \ge 1$ the following are equivalent, so a program may test whichever is cheapest:

1. Connected and acyclic.
2. Connected and has $n - 1$ edges.
3. Acyclic and has $n - 1$ edges.
4. Exactly one simple path between every pair of vertices.
5. Connected, and deleting any edge disconnects it.
6. Acyclic, and adding any missing edge creates a cycle.

The practical test is (2): reject the input unless it has $n - 1$ edges, then one BFS must visit every vertex. No separate cycle check is required, because a connected graph with $n - 1$ edges cannot contain a cycle. The count alone is not enough. The graph with edges $1\!-\!2$, $2\!-\!4$, $1\!-\!4$, $3\!-\!5$ has $n - 1 = 4$ edges, a cycle, and a second component. Fewer than $n - 1$ edges cannot be connected. A tree of $n \ge 2$ has a leaf; peel that leaf and its edge, and $n - 1$ peels leave one vertex, so the edge count is exactly $n - 1$. A self-loop or a repeated edge spends one of those edges and joins nothing new. A single vertex is a tree. The empty graph is not; LeetCode's `root = nullptr` is still a legal input of the pointer problems below.

