The calls of `size` on a complete tree of six nodes, in the order they **return**. A hollow node has been called but has not returned yet; the amber node has just returned, and the number under it is its answer. A parent can only finish after both of its children, which is why the answers climb from the leaves to the root.

:::

On a general tree the recursive walk is `dfs(u, parent)`, and the parent argument is how you avoid walking back up the edge you arrived on. The iterative form, which is the one to submit when $n$ can be $10^5$ and the tree can be a path, is a BFS (or an explicit stack) that records an order, then a reverse scan for anything that flows upward. A frame typically costs tens to a couple of hundred bytes. A recursion of depth $10^4$ is fine on most judges. A default stack of a few megabytes can overflow on a path of $10^5$. Some judges raise the stack limit to the memory limit, and there a recursion of depth $2 \cdot 10^5$ usually fits, but you cannot count on it everywhere. An explicit stack does not depend on that gift. Push the right child before the left so the left is popped first; reversing a root–right–left walk is a postorder.

The four orders on $B$ are a useful checksum: preorder `0 1 4 7 8 5 2 6 9`, inorder `7 4 8 1 5 0 2 9 6`, postorder `7 8 4 5 1 9 6 2 0`, level order `0 1 2 4 5 6 7 8 9`. With the leaf $3$ restored, the preorder of $R$ ends in $3$ and the postorder places $3$ just before the root.

## Lesson 5: reading the input {#sec-tr-read}

Contest trees are $1$-indexed. Allocate length $n + 1$ and ignore index $0$. Rebuild the arrays inside the test: zeroing a global of size $2 \cdot 10^5$ on each of $10^4$ tests is $10^{10}$ writes. When $n = 1$ the input has no edge lines; a loop of $n - 1$ iterations reads nothing. Subtract $1$ from both endpoints of an edge, and from each parent, when the arrays are $0$-based. The $1$-indexed parents of $R$ rooted at vertex $1$ (labels one larger than the picture) are $p_2, \ldots, p_n = 1\,1\,1\,2\,2\,3\,5\,5\,7$.

A parent array is a tree when it has exactly one root (the entry $-1$, or the vertex that never appears, depending on the format), every parent index is in range and is not the vertex itself, and walking up from every vertex reaches that root. Anything left over is a cycle. `[-1, 0, 3, 2]` cycles. `[-1, -1, 0, 1]` has two roots. `[2, -1, 1, 1, 3]` is a tree.

