## Pattern 2: comparing and mirroring {#sec-tr-p2}

**From one tree to two.** Pattern 1 asked each subtree for one number. Comparing asks a **pair** of subtrees a yes/no question, so the recursion takes two nodes and steps in both trees at once. A mirror changes only which children are paired.

**Recognise it.** Two trees, or one tree and its mirror. "Same", "symmetric", "invert", "flip equivalent", "is this tree a subtree of that one". The walk takes one step in each tree at the same time.

::: {.callout-important title="Template"}
Both nodes null: equal. Exactly one null: not equal. Otherwise the values match and the paired recursive calls match. A mirror swaps which child is paired: left with right, right with left.
:::

**Invariant.** The call on a pair reports the comparison of those two subtrees, and of no other nodes.

**Complexity.** $O(n)$ time, $O(h)$ stack, where $n$ is the size of the smaller tree once a mismatch returns. Comparing every subtree of one tree against the other is $O(n \cdot m)$ and is a different problem (subtree of another tree).

