## Pattern 4: parent arrays {#sec-tr-p4}

**From pointers to an array.** Patterns 1–3 had `left` and `right`. Contest inputs often give only $p_i$. The two directions survive — values that flow down from the root and values that add up from the leaves — but the order now comes from a BFS instead of the call stack. @fig-tr-anim-bfs-sizes runs the bottom-up half.

**Recognise it.** The input is $p_i$, "manager", "boss", or a list of employees. Vertices are $1..n$ or $0..n-1$. There is no left and right.

::: {.callout-important title="Template"}
Build children lists in one pass. BFS from the root gives a top-down order: a parent appears before its children, so depth and "new parent" can be written forward. The reverse of that order is bottom-up: subtree sizes add after the children are finished. Walking up the parent array from every vertex is $O(n \cdot h)$ and times out on a path of $10^5$.
:::

**Invariant.** After the forward pass, every vertex stores a value that depends only on its ancestors. After the reverse pass, every vertex stores a value that depends only on its subtree.

**Complexity.** $O(n)$ time and memory when each vertex is entered a constant number of times. $p_i = i - 1$ is a path; recursion of depth $n$ is the wrong tool. Four formats show up: $p_2,\ldots,p_n$ with $p_i < i$ (labels are already top-down), a `parent[i]` array in any order with root $-1$, a manager array plus a head id, and parent–child pairs whose root is the parent that never appears as a child. Do not scan $v = 0, 1, 2, \ldots$ unless every parent has a smaller label. `[-1, 0, 4, 0, 1]` sets the parent of $2$ to $4$; a forward scan reads `depth[4]` before it is written. A memoised walk-up fills each chain from the first known vertex downward, once per vertex, and stays $O(n)$ on a path.

::: {#4e1758b9 .cell execution_count=48}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def depths_memo(parent):
    """Edge-depths. parent labels may be larger than the child."""
    n = len(parent)
    depth = [-1] * n
    depth[parent.index(-1)] = 0

    def fill(v):
        chain = []
        while depth[v] < 0:
            chain.append(v)
            v = parent[v]
        d = depth[v]
        for u in reversed(chain):
            d += 1
            depth[u] = d

    for v in range(n):
        if depth[v] < 0:
            fill(v)
    return depth


assert depths_memo([-1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 2, 4, 4, 6]) == [0, 1, 1, 1, 2, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3]
assert depths_memo([-1, 0, 4, 0, 1]) == [0, 1, 3, 1, 2]
```
