## Pattern 6: rooting an edge list {#sec-tr-p6}

**From no root to the right root.** Degrees cannot answer a distance or a spread from one vertex. But any vertex of a tree can serve as the root, so choose the one the question is about, and one traversal answers it.

**Recognise it.** The input is an edge list, and the question is a distance, a path, or "how long until this spreads". The edges are not yet parent and child.

::: {.callout-important title="Template"}
Root at the vertex the question is about. One BFS, or any other traversal, writes `dist[v] = dist[parent] + weight`: a tree has one path, so a general-graph shortest-path algorithm is unnecessary. Every later query that is forced through that root has length `dist[x] + dist[y]`, which reuses an edge when the two paths share a prefix and is then longer than the direct distance. A pointer tree has no parent link upward: build a parent map in one walk, then the three neighbours of a node are left, right and parent. Several special vertices mean one traversal each.
:::

**Invariant.** After the BFS, `dist[v]` is the distance from the chosen root to $v$, because a tree has one path.

**Complexity.** $O(n + q)$ for one root and $q$ queries. One search per query is $O(nq)$ and fails at $n = q = 10^5$. Store the distances in 64-bit integers: weights of $10^9$ on a path of length $10^5$ exceed $2^{31}$.

