**Looking back.** Six patterns, one recursion. A value either comes **up** from the children (measure, compare, subtree sizes) or goes **down** from the parent (path sums, depths, distances), and the real decision is the order that makes the needed values ready: the call stack on pointers, a BFS order on a parent array, or a root you choose yourself. Degrees are the one case where no order is needed at all. @sec-tv makes those orders the subject.

## Pattern map

| Signal in the statement | Pattern | Section |
|---|---|---|
| One number per subtree: size, height, sum, perfect | Measure on the way up | @sec-tr-p1 |
| Same shape, mirror, symmetric, flip | Walk two nodes together | @sec-tr-p2 |
| Root-to-leaf sum, the paths as strings, a number read down the path | State passed down | @sec-tr-p3 |
| $p_i$, boss, manager, subtree size of a general tree | Parent array, children, BFS order | @sec-tr-p4 |
| $n - 1$ edges, and the question is a star, leaves, or an edge labelling | Degrees, no root | @sec-tr-p5 |
| Distance, a path, or a spread from one vertex, on an edge list | Root there, one traversal | @sec-tr-p6 |
| Closest ancestor with a property, subordinates, inform time | Parent array, memoised walk-up | @sec-tr-p4 |
| Label or colour the edges, or turn the tree into a star | Degrees and leaf-neighbours | @sec-tr-p5 |

"Maximum depth" of a tree given as an edge list is a BFS from the given root, not a `TreeNode` recursion. A path between two arbitrary vertices, rather than a root-to-leaf path, needs the fork of the two paths and is the lowest-common-ancestor chapter.

## Pitfalls

::: {.callout-warning title="Common mistakes"}
- Counting edges in a problem whose sample says a single node has depth $1$, or counting nodes in a problem whose sample says that node has height $0$.
- `1 + min(left, right)` when a missing child returns $0$. The right spine then has minimum depth $1$.
- Treating $n - 1$ edges as a tree without the connectivity check.
- Reading `u v` as "parent $u$, child $v$" on an undirected edge list.
- A second recursive call on the same child, which is $O(n^2)$ on a path.
- Recursion of depth $n$ on a parent array $p_i = i - 1$. Use the BFS order.
- Forgetting the parent when infection or distance spreads to every neighbour.
- `1 << k` in C++ for a perfect tree taller than $31$ levels. This chapter's complete-tree constraint stops at $16$.
- Clearing a global array of size $2 \cdot 10^5$ inside each of $10^4$ tests.
- Stopping a path-sum search because the remaining target became negative.
- Scanning parent labels in order $0, 1, 2, \ldots$ when a parent has a larger label, as in `[-1, 0, 4, 0, 1]`.
- Running a general-graph shortest-path algorithm on a tree.
- Printing edge labels in traversal order when the statement asks for the input order.
:::

