::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="[M] The path from X to Y"}
[AC abc270_c · Simple path](https://atcoder.jp/contests/abc270/tasks/abc270_c). Root at $Y$. Then $X$, its parent, and so on, is the path in the required order. On edges $1-2$, $2-3$, $2-4$, the path from $4$ to $3$ is $4, 2, 3$.

::: {#b1a35362 .cell execution_count=95}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def path_xy(n, edges, x, y):
    adj = [[] for _ in range(n + 1)]
    for u, v in edges:
        adj[u].append(v)
        adj[v].append(u)
    parent = [0] * (n + 1)
    parent[y] = -1
    order = [y]
    head = 0
    while head < len(order):
        u = order[head]
        head += 1
        for v in adj[u]:
            if parent[v] == 0:
                parent[v] = u
                order.append(v)
    out = []
    v = x
    while v != -1:
        out.append(v)
        v = parent[v]
    return out

assert path_xy(4, [(1, 2), (2, 3), (2, 4)], 4, 3) == [4, 2, 3]
```
:::


