::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="[M] A star that avoids forbidden midpoints"}
[CF 1583B · Omkar and Heavenly Tree](https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/1583/B){rating="1200"}. Fewer than $n$ rules means some vertex is never a forbidden midpoint, and the star centred there puts only that vertex in the middle of a path. With $n = 4$ and one rule whose midpoint is $2$, the centre is $1$.

::: {#196eacf5 .cell execution_count=97}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def heavenly_star(n, rules):
    forbidden = [False] * (n + 1)
    for _a, b, _c in rules:
        forbidden[b] = True
    centre = 1
    while forbidden[centre]:
        centre += 1
    return centre, [(centre, v) for v in range(1, n + 1) if v != centre]

assert heavenly_star(4, [(1, 2, 3)]) == (1, [(1, 2), (1, 3), (1, 4)])
```
:::


