::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="[H] Recover the forest from degrees and XORs"}
[CF 501C · Misha and Forest](https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/501/C){rating="1500"}. Peel the leaves: a degree-$1$ vertex names its only neighbour in the XOR. Degrees `[1, 2, 1]` and XORs `[1, 2, 1]` recover the path $0-1-2$. Two leaves joined to each other, degrees `[1, 1]` and XORs `[1, 0]`, emit that one edge and then stop.

::: {#de080d11 .cell execution_count=98}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def recover_forest(deg, xor):
    deg, xor = deg[:], xor[:]
    queue = [v for v in range(len(deg)) if deg[v] == 1]
    edges = []
    head = 0
    while head < len(queue):
        v = queue[head]
        head += 1
        if deg[v] != 1:
            continue
        u = xor[v]
        edges.append((v, u))
        deg[v] = 0
        deg[u] -= 1
        xor[u] ^= v
        if deg[u] == 1:
            queue.append(u)
    return edges

assert recover_forest([1, 2, 1], [1, 2, 1]) == [(0, 1), (2, 1)]
assert recover_forest([1, 1], [1, 0]) == [(0, 1)]
```
:::


:::

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="[H] Leaves from the degree sequence"}
In a tree, $\sum_v (\deg v - 2) = -2$, so the number of leaves is $2 + \sum_{\deg v \ge 3}(\deg v - 2)$. Three vertices of degree $4$, two of degree $3$ and five of degree $2$ force $10$ leaves and $20$ vertices. The degree sequence does not determine the tree.

::: {#1dc01dc2 .cell execution_count=99}
``` {.python .cell-code}
high = [4, 4, 4, 3, 3]
twos = 5
leaves = 2 + sum(d - 2 for d in high)
vertices = len(high) + twos + leaves
hand = sum(high) + twos * 2 + leaves
assert leaves == 10 and vertices == 20
assert hand == 38 == 2 * (vertices - 1)
```
:::


